{"kind":"assert","text":"SIMPLIFICATION / CRUFT AUDIT (addressed to builder, from checker). Whole-project review at main head 1a26954. Behavior-preserving cleanups only; none add a dependency, change RuntimeID, weaken confinement, or alter the revision digest, except the two flagged. Ordered by impact. Two independent passes agreed on the top items; I spot-verified the dead-code and bug claims.\n\nNOTE: one item here is a real BUG, not cleanup — see item 3 (receiver err-shadowing); it is written up in full in the security audit as finding S2. Fixing it falls naturally out of the suggested refactor.\n\n1. SQLite open + defensive-config boilerplate is duplicated 5 times (highest impact). The identical DBCONFIG_DEFENSIVE / TRUSTED_SCHEMA / ENABLE_LOAD_EXTENSION triple, plus the file DSN construction and SetMaxOpenConns(1), appears in internal/inbox/inbox.go (~93-113), internal/definition/registry.go (~57-77, byte-identical to inbox and opening the SAME control.sqlite the inbox already opened), internal/projection/projection.go (~145-168), internal/profile/compiler.go buildScratch (~786-801), and internal/query/query.go (~142-150, raw *sqlite3.Conn variant). Extract a tiny internal package, e.g. Configure(*sqlite3.Conn) error for the three Config calls (usable by the raw-conn site too) and Open(dsn, pragmas...) (*sql.DB, error) for url.URL + Configure + pool + pragmas, keeping per-site PRAGMA lists as parameters. Net ~40 lines and one place to change the confinement posture (today a fix must land in five files). While there: trusted_schema/foreign_keys are set two or three times per DB (a DBCONFIG plus one or two PRAGMAs) — keep one layer; and filepath.Abs is applied inconsistently (registry/query do it, inbox/projection do not) — pick one and put it in the helper.\n\n2. Sorted-map-keys helper duplicated 4 times, two of them hand-rolled insertion sorts. profile/source.go sortedKeys (uses sort.Strings, good); projection.go sortedKeys (identical, but calls a hand-written insertion sort literally named slicesSort); engine.go sortedProjectionKeys (third, non-generic copy, the only reason engine imports sort); registry.go draftDigest (inlines keys+sort.Strings a fourth time). query.go sortSchemas is a second hand-written insertion sort over NamespaceResult.Alias. Consolidate to one sortedKeys[V any] (using slices.Sort or sort.Strings — both already used in the repo) in a shared internal package; replace sortSchemas with slices.SortFunc. Determinism is unaffected (all inputs are unique map keys/aliases). Also: profile.digestSources and definition.draftDigest are the same 12-line digest algorithm differing only in a RuntimeID prefix, and the literal \"sha256:\"+hex.EncodeToString(...) idiom appears at ~7 sites (source.go, registry.go x3, control.go, canonical.go) while profile.digestJSON already does exactly that — one shared digest.Of / digest.SortedMap collapses them. CAUTION: digestSources computes the pinned revision identity; the exact byte stream (RuntimeID, then per sorted key: 0x00, key, 0x00, content) must be preserved — validate against the revision-stability test in compiler_test.go.\n\n3. Dead code (pure deletions, verified where noted). profile/types.go Profile.Expression is exported with ZERO callers (verified) and duplicates the private program() lookup in evaluate.go — delete it, and while there simplify program()'s ad-hoc anonymous-interface return to (Program, *jsonata.Expression, bool). definition/registry.go Registry.Activate has ZERO non-test callers (verified; activation goes through BeginActivation/FinishActivation) and its body duplicates the UPDATE in FinishActivation — delete it. query.go relationColumns' exportsOnly parameter is always false at its single call site, so the if guard is always taken and allColumns() is a named return-nil — drop both. compiler.go's viewSQL map is written only to have its keys read in nameExists, which could iterate profile.Views instead (as isView already does) — delete the field. Smaller crumbs: cli.go var result any is declared, never assigned, and discarded with _ = result; evaluate.go re-checks !program.Normalizer inside a loop after an early return on the same condition; engine.projectionPath re-derives activationPaths' first return; canonical.go uintText is optionalUint with a different return type; compiler.go computeDigests-adjacent duplicateName(columns) is called twice in one branch.\n\n4. THE RECEIVER err-SHADOWING BUG (see security S2). receiver.go ServeHTTP: `if err := decodeOTLP(...); err == nil { records, err = flatten...(...) }` assigns the flatten error to the inner shadowed err, so the post-switch `if err != nil` (outer err, nil) never fires and flatten failures return HTTP 200 with zero records. Refactoring the three near-identical signal cases into a per-path flatten-func lookup with a single outer `records, err := flatten(body, mediaType)` both removes ~12 duplicated lines AND fixes the bug.\n\n5. Twin quote-aware scanners hasUnquotedAsterisk and hasUnquotedRange (compiler.go) are byte-identical state machines differing only in the terminal case (`*` vs `..`). Fold into one scanUnquoted(source, hit func) used by both. ~28 lines. Low risk but this is part of the untrusted-input gate — keep the escape rule (current=='\\\\' \u0026\u0026 quote!='`') and the admitted-character set exactly, and keep the asterisk-predicate comment.\n\n6. Nullable-pointer conversion is open-coded ~10 times with a name collision: inbox.nullableString(sql.NullString) *string vs definition.nullableString(string) any — same name, opposite meaning (the latter maps \"\"-\u003eSQL NULL) — plus repeated `if x.Valid { p = \u0026x.String }` blocks in scanApp, ActivationJournals, Frontier, Stats, and the inverse pointerValue in projection.go. Go 1.26 has generic sql.Null[T]; declare fields as sql.Null[string]/sql.Null[int64] with one shared ptr[T](sql.Null[T]) *T, and rename definition.nullableString so the collision stops. ~25 lines and a real clarity gain.\n\n7. json.NewDecoder(...).UseNumber() decode is repeated 6 times (projection.go x2, evaluate.go x3, cli.go). A small decodeNumeric(data, target) helper per package (or one shared) removes ~15 lines and makes the \"UseNumber must be preserved for numeric determinism\" invariant harder to forget.\n\n8. engine.compile imports testing/fstest into the PRODUCTION binary (engine.go) purely to build a MapFS to feed profile.Load, while profile.loadSources exists only to turn an fs.FS back into a map AND re-implements ValidateSourceElement's checks almost line-for-line. Add profile.Compile(name, map[string][]byte) that validates each element via ValidateSourceElement, checks MaxSourceBytes, then compiles; Load becomes walk-into-map + Compile; engine.compile and the testing/fstest+io/fs imports disappear. ~20 lines and testing out of the shipped binary. CAUTION: the revision digest is over the same map so revisions are unchanged, but validate against compiler_test.go and embed_test.go, and note error-report ordering for oversized source may shift (loadSources currently enforces per-file limits during the walk).\n\n9. Smaller consistency items: four regexes are compiled per-call inside hot functions (compiler.go parseColumns per-column loop, compileRead, validateQueryShape x2) while 25 siblings sit in the package var block — hoist them. evaluate.go's table-changes loop ranges over a literal map, making the reported error on malformed output nondeterministic (and needing a switch to re-route rows) — a fixed-order slice removes ~8 lines and makes error reporting deterministic. sqliteValue(value, logical any) round-trips a typed value through fmt.Sprint twice — type the parameter as profile.LogicalType. control.errorCode uses strings.Contains for frontier_changed/query_budget_exceeded amid six errors.Is cases — export query sentinels and switch to errors.Is (keep the message text byte-identical, it is documented in mcp.md). statusProfile takes ctx as its second parameter against the repo's ctx-first convention. RemoveElement validates a path via ValidateSourceElement(path, []byte(\"x\")) with dummy content — split the path-profile check out. Bundled-app name list is stated twice in embed.go (Load switch vs Names()).\n\n10. Test/example crumbs: engine_test.go repeats the create-\u003eValidate-\u003eActivate(\"reset\",true) sequence seven times and the eventTime+raw-record fixture three times (a mustActivateBundle helper removes ~60 lines); mcp/server_test.go hand-rolls contains (slices.Contains); tailapps/embed_test.go hand-rolls cloneMap (maps.Clone); compiler_test.go's \"invalid splitter inputs\" slice includes one VALID statement then excludes it in the assertion, so that element tests nothing.\n\nOVERALL: this is an unusually disciplined codebase — crisp package responsibilities, small exported surfaces, deliberately-commented security constructs, almost no speculative abstraction. The cruft is one dominant species: cross-package copy-paste of small utilities (SQLite hardening x5, sorted-keys x4, digest-format x~7, nullable helpers x10), a side effect of keeping packages mutually dependency-free; one small shared internal utility package removes the bulk of it at near-zero risk. Items 1-8 remove roughly 200 lines from the ~6000-line non-test codebase and, via item 4, fix one silently-swallowed-error bug and (item 9, evaluate.go loop) one nondeterministic-error-reporting wart. Only items 2 (digest) and 8 (Compile) touch determinism-adjacent code and must be validated against the existing revision-stability tests."}